experiment: tutorwide
  instrument        tutorwide   trial seed 0000000001355b54   1214 trials per generation
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  tutorwide -- `tutorchain` showed the convention decaying to nothing over
  five generations. Was that the CHANNEL losing information, or just that a
  tutor says less than it was asked and each pupil inherits a smaller task?
  the three chains
    plain       each generation's tape is its tutor's production, as before
    wide        the tutor's own vowel CENTRE and its own ORDER, re-spread
                to the host's log distance -- the MAPPING propagates and the
                WIDTH is held fixed. The ORDER is the whole point: without
                it the tape re-imposes a mapping instead of inheriting one.
    wide-flip   the same, with the two labels exchanged at EVERY step
  why the third the widened target always carries a wide distinction, so a
                pupil could produce one with NO information from its tutor.
                The only inherited thing is WHICH VOWEL IS HIGH, and the
                flip is what tests that it was inherited at all.
  the bars      fixed in advance. WIDTH if `wide` clears 3 SE at generation 4
                where `plain` does not, AND `wide-flip` alternates sign --
                then the channel is clean and the limit is what a creature
                can SAY. LEAK if `wide` decays too. CONFOUNDED if `wide`
                holds but `wide-flip` does NOT alternate, because then the
                width is producing the distinction and nothing is inherited.
  18 chains, 5 generations, three chains per creature.


  generation  plain chain            WIDE chain             wide, labels flipped  
  0            +96.1 +/-   5.1 Hz     +96.1 +/-   5.1 Hz    (shared)              
  1            +25.6 +/-   7.6 Hz     +61.0 +/-  11.3 Hz     -54.9 +/-   9.7 Hz   
  2            +10.5 +/-   5.4 Hz     +31.8 +/-  20.8 Hz     +55.1 +/-  11.7 Hz   
  3             -0.3 +/-   3.7 Hz     +44.2 +/-  18.1 Hz     -56.7 +/-  14.5 Hz   
  4             +0.5 +/-   3.1 Hz     +35.5 +/-  19.7 Hz     +61.9 +/-  16.3 Hz   

  DID THE LAST LESSON LAND   generation 4, wide chain:  +0.1258 +/- 0.0047  (26.6 SE)

  ALTERNATION ON THE WIDE-FLIP CHAIN, per generation (predicted -,+,-,+)
    generation 1    -54.9 +/-   9.7 Hz   predicted -   MATCHES
    generation 2    +55.1 +/-  11.7 Hz   predicted +   MATCHES
    generation 3    -56.7 +/-  14.5 Hz   predicted -   MATCHES
    generation 4    +61.9 +/-  16.3 Hz   predicted +   MATCHES

  wide holds at gen 4    no
  plain dead at gen 4    YES
  wide-flip alternates   YES (4 of 4 resolved generations)

  IT LEAKS. Even with the width held fixed and only the mapping passed
  on, the widened chain is within 3 SE of zero at generation 4
  (+35.5 +/- 19.7 Hz). So the decay is not explained by tutors being
  quiet: something is lost in transmission itself, and handing each pupil
  a full-width target does not rescue it.

  THAT IS THE STRONGER NEGATIVE OF THE TWO, and it closes the obvious
  repair. What it does NOT identify is WHAT is lost -- the mapping is one
  bit per generation and a chain that cannot carry one bit is a different
  and sharper problem than a chain that narrows.
